The microstructure was first examined using SEM and EBSD analyses. The microstructure of the Q sample consists of homogeneous martensite with a hardness of approximately 450 HV1, as confirmed by both SEM ( Fig. 7 
                         a) and EBSD ( Fig. 7 b and c). The grain misorientation distribution is also characteristic of martensitic structures ( Fig. 8 
                         ). Low-angle (<15°) ( Fig. 8 b) and high-angle (>55°) ( Fig. 8 d) boundaries, associated with increased dislocation density [ 61 ] and the mutual orientation of martensitic laths [ 62 ], respectively, are frequently observed. After tempering ( Fig. 7 b), the hardness decreases by approximately 150 HV1 to about 300 HV1, and the characteristic martensitic morphology is no longer observed, while precipitates become clearly visible. They are predominantly located at prior martensite grain boundaries. EBSD analysis did not enable the detection of precipitates, and only a negligible fraction of RA grains was identified. However, in the unique grain color map (UGC) ( Fig. 7 f), small sub-grains can be observed in larger alpha phase grains. Their increased number compared with Q sample ( Fig. 7 c) may suggest that they may be precipitates. The grain misorientation distribution differs very slightly compared to the Q sample. Both low-angle (<15°) and high-angle (>55°) boundaries are less frequent, while some grains separated by ∼45° boundaries, corresponding to α/γ interfaces [ 63 ], are present ( Fig. 8 c).

Further investigations were performed using TEM ( Fig. 10 
                         ). The quenching step for all samples, besides achieving a fully martensitic transformation, was also intended to homogenize their chemical composition. As expected, no clear redistribution of chemical elements is observed in Q sample ( Fig. 10 a), and the measured Mn concentration is about 5 wt% ( Fig. 11 
                         a). In contrast, significant redistribution of chemical elements occurs during tempering, as shown in  Fig. 10 b. It can be seen that the formed carbides are primarily enriched in C and Mn. The latter reaches concentrations exceeding 20 wt% ( Fig. 11 a), while the matrix is depleted in Mn compared to the Q sample. Precipitates are depleted in Al, and a slight depletion in Si can also be observed. After intercritical annealing at 710 °C for 40 s, a clear chemical redistribution is observed in the initially quenched sample ( Fig. 10 c). In the case of C, the redistribution is not clearly visible; however, an increase in Mn concentration up to about 9 wt% is measured in the austenitic regions ( Fig. 11 b). This clearly demonstrates that even a relatively short annealing time of 40 s is sufficient to enable advanced Mn diffusion and substantial redistribution between the α and γ phases. Additionally, the RA is visibly depleted in Al but also in Si. In the case of the sample annealed after tempering ( Fig. 10 d), a similar situation is observed; however, spherical regions with significantly increased C and Mn content are visible. Previous SEM results indicate that these may correspond to undissolved carbide precipitates. However, comparison of  Fig. 10 b and d reveals a significant reduction in the number and size of precipitates, indicating their partial dissolution during intercritical annealing and the associated release of C and Mn into the surrounding austenite. TEM-EDS linear scans ( Fig. 11 b) show that the Mn redistribution is not uniform across different grains, varying from approximately 8 to over 16 wt% depending on the proximity to precipitates. Chemical inhomogeneity within individual grains is more pronounced in sample T 710, where a Mn-enriched shell and a core with a lower Mn concentration are frequently observed ( Fig. 11 b).
